{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaef0977\" lines=\"25-41\">\n<description>\nReplace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.\n- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.\n\nRule:\n- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.\n- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position\n  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.\n- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.\n\nKey insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the\ndistance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that\nexcludes the orange background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"305b1341\" lines=\"22-37\">\n<description>\nExpand each sparse color object into a filled rectangular region using its legend-paired fill color.\n\nInput structure\nA background-color grid has a two-column legend in columns 0 and 1, starting at row 0: each row gives\na source color in the left column and that source color's fill color in the right column.  Outside the\nlegend, the foreground pixels are sparse dot objects whose colors are exactly the legend source colors.\n\nRule\nClear the legend.  For each source color, take the bbox of all of its non-legend dots, expand that bbox\nby one pixel in every direction, and fill background cells inside the expanded rectangle with the paired\nlegend fill color while preserving every original non-legend dot.\n\nKey insight\nThe legend order is also the layer order: earlier legend rows are in front, so fill rectangles are painted\nfrom the last legend row back to the first and higher-priority fills overwrite lower-priority fills.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7955b3c\" lines=\"386-403\">\n<description>\nReplace gray plus-shaped damage masks with the hidden colors of the rectilinear mosaic.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color 8 appears only as isolated 3x3 plus masks.\n- The non-mask pixels are visible parts of a clean grid made from horizontal\n  and vertical color regions.\n\nRule:\n- Treat every color-8 pixel as unknown damage and leave all other pixels fixed.\n- First copy unknown pixels from exact matching parallel rows or columns.\n- Then fill any remaining mask cells from opposite-side continuations and the\n  better-supported local straight edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The gray pluses are occluders, not objects; the underlying colors are\n  determined by repeated line templates and nearby axis-aligned boundaries.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d6c6e23\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nMove each vertical stream of pixels into a bottom-aligned centered pile.\n\nInput structure\n- A mostly background grid contains one or more vertical source columns of\n  foreground pixels.\n- Each source column has n^2 foreground pixels, and the top-to-bottom color\n  sequence splits into monochrome groups of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nRule\n- Clear the canvas to background.\n- For each source column, read the foreground colors from top to bottom and\n  rebuild them at the bottom below the same column as odd-width centered\n  rows of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered vertical pixels are falling grains; a square number of\n  grains decomposes into the odd row widths of a centered pyramid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b25e450b\" lines=\"28-43\">\n<description>\nSlide each black border rectangle to the opposite border, recoloring its swept stripe orange and leaving the rectangle black at the far edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains black rectangular 4-connected masks, each touching exactly one outer border.\n- Grey cells form incidental pattern content; orange is the trail color used when a black mask slides.\n\nRule\n- Treat all black rectangles as moving simultaneously.\n- A rectangle touching the top or bottom edge moves vertically to the opposite edge in the same columns.\n- A rectangle touching the left or right edge moves horizontally to the opposite edge in the same rows.\n- Recolor the complete swept row or column stripe to orange, then stamp every destination rectangle in black.\n\nKey insight\n- Edge contact gives the slide direction; the output is the set of opposite-edge black rectangles plus their orange trails.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25e02866\" lines=\"14-31\">\n<description>\nMerge same-sized filled rectangular objects by overlaying their internal marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background surrounds two or more separated 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every object is a filled rectangle of the same size.\n- Each rectangle has the same majority base color plus one or more non-base marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Create one output rectangle with the shared object size, filled with the shared base color.\n- For each input rectangle, copy every non-base marker pixel to the same relative location\n  in the output.\n- Marker overlays must either land on base color or agree in color with an existing marker.\n\nKey insight:\nThe separated rectangles are layers of the same template; their colored marker pixels\ncombine by local coordinates while the repeated base fill is just the output canvas.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"abbfd121\" lines=\"56-74\">\n<description>\nRecover the repeating background pattern hidden under the largest